@page "/agent-avatar"

<CatHead Title="Agent avatars">
    Who an agent is: a sphere lit from inside, in one of six identity tones, drawn in CSS with no image
    and no script, so it is there in the first paint and follows the theme. The tone stays fixed for an
    agent; its state changes how the sphere moves, never its colour. For an agent with a face, see
    <a href="/mochi">Mochi</a>.
</CatHead>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.AgentAvatar.Roster" Title="A roster of agents" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <code>.agent-avatar</code> wherever a person's <a href="/avatar"><code>.avatar</code></a> would
        go: a list row, a chat message, a card head. It is never the only thing that names the agent
        &mdash; the name beside it is &mdash; so <code>aria-label</code> repeats the name for a reader
        who lands on the image alone. The <a href="/agent-status">status pill</a> says what the agent is
        doing; the sphere only moves with it.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.AgentAvatar.Tones" Title="Tones" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <code>data-tone</code> picks one of six: <code>sedna</code>, the default, in the two colours of
        the mark; <code>aurora</code>, <code>ember</code>, <code>orbit</code>, <code>nebula</code> and
        <code>glacier</code>. <strong>Give each agent one tone and keep it</strong>: the tone is how a
        reader tells two agents apart in a long thread, so it is identity, not state.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.AgentAvatar.States" Title="States" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <code>data-state</code> changes the motion: <code>thinking</code> turns faster,
        <code>working</code> turns faster still inside a ring, <code>listening</code> and <code>speaking</code>
        follow <code>--level</code>, and <code>offline</code> is grey and still. With no state the
        sphere turns slowly. Under <code>prefers-reduced-motion</code> every state is still, and the
        ring and glow stay as the cue.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>--level</code>, from 0 to 1, is the number the app's audio code writes &mdash; on the
        avatar or any element above it, so one write on a <a href="/voice">voice session</a> moves the
        avatar, the bars and the mic button together. The two here are fixed so the page holds still.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.AgentAvatar.Sizes" Title="Sizes" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <code>.agent-avatar--xs</code> (20px), <code>--sm</code> (24px), the bare
        <code>.agent-avatar</code> (32px), <code>--lg</code> (40px), <code>--xl</code> (56px),
        <code>--2xl</code> (96px) and <code>--hero</code> (200px).
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.AgentAvatar.WithPeople" Title="Beside people" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        In an <a href="/avatar"><code>.avatar-group</code></a>, write <code>class="avatar
        agent-avatar"</code>: the group's size, overlap and cut-out apply, and the sphere fills the
        circle. An agent on a ticket is then counted with the people on it.
    </p>
</CatExample>

<CatExample TExample="Examples.AgentAvatar.Hero" Title="Where the agent is the page" Demo="ex-demo--block ex-demo--pad">
    <p>
        <code>.agent-avatar--hero</code> for a voice call or a first-run screen, where the agent is the
        one thing to look at.
    </p>
</CatExample>
